The current position has exactly seven empty cells: one each in columns 0, 1 and 5, plus four stacked in column 6. Exhaustive enumeration of every 4-in-a-row window shows that the only remaining winning lines are:

- o wins horizontally on row 0, columns 3-6 if and only if o occupies both (0,5) and (0,6);
- x wins if and only if x occupies (2,6) (the unique unblocked window that still belongs to x).

Consequently x can win only by playing the second stone of column 6, while o wins by occupying column 5 together with the fourth stone of column 6.

Playing column 6 immediately hands x the second stone of that column and loses at once. Playing a dummy column (0 or 1) allows x to seize column 5 and thereafter hold the draw. The unique move that occupies the critical cell (0,5) at once is column 5. After that placement the remaining six cells consist of two dummy columns plus the four-high stack. In every possible continuation:

- o never starts the stack (doing so would give x an immediate win);
- therefore x is forced to start it;
- o answers by taking every even stone of the stack, including the fourth stone (0,6).

The two o-stones on row 0 together with the two stones already present produce four-in-a-row for o on the final move. No interleaving of dummy moves lets x obtain (2,6) or prevent o from obtaining (0,6). Hence column 5 is a forced win.

Final Answer: 5

